Q: Is it possible to make a &quot;write-only&quot; object? A: <p>This is not possible with TypeScript. Properties can be made read-only with the <code>readonly</code> modifier or <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/utility-types.html#readonlytype" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the <code>Readonly</code> utility type</a> but they cannot be marked as write-only on a type level.</p>
<p>What you can use instead is a method that sets properties but no properties are exposed.</p>
<p>Using a wrapper class:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>class WriteOnly&lt;K extends PropertyKey, V&gt; {
  private _myValues: Partial&lt;Record&lt;K, V&gt;&gt; = {};

  public setValue(key: K, value: V) {
    this._myValues[key] = value;
  }
}

const foo = new WriteOnly&lt;string, string&gt;();

foo.setValue(&quot;bar&quot;, &quot;yes&quot;); // the only thing allowed
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?#-agUwkA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Playground Link</a></p>
<p>Or implemented with a <code>Map</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>class WriteOnly&lt;K extends PropertyKey, V&gt; {
  private _myValues: Map&lt;K, V&gt; = new Map&lt;K, V&gt;();

  public setValue(key: K, value: V) {
    this._myValues.set(key, value);
  }
}

const foo = new WriteOnly();

foo.setValue(&quot;bar&quot;, &quot;yes&quot;); // the only thing allowed
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?#code/MYGwhgzhAEDqBOBLALgUwPIDsQE8A8A0tKgB5qYAmMACvAPYAOq8yOBqOANNAGoB80AN4AoaNAZIAbmDTQA+gFscPMCACuqCAC5oAWTANC3ftAC80TKgDueg0d58AFAEoA3MNHi1AIxCJg0BCoyCrqqI4A1hw6BNzSYTo8zkKeYsgAFogQAHSKyqoaOUHIkRxxBahungC+wrXCwHSYEMjQAGZ0dGYW1nBIaFi4Lu7CHXTZxaEajgBE3mDwM9wzOJozbtAA9JvQGajQTbi7mZgA5tCqIHRWqBTCQA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Playground Link</a></p>
<p>Alternatively, you can use a Map directly and strip away anything that can change the values inside. This is easily achievable on the type level but you need a function that returns the new Map:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>type WriteOnlyMap&lt;K, V&gt; = Pick&lt;Map&lt;K, V&gt;, &quot;set&quot; | &quot;delete&quot; | &quot;clear&quot;&gt;;

function createWriteOnlyMap&lt;K, V&gt;(): WriteOnlyMap&lt;K, V&gt; { 
  return new Map&lt;K, V&gt;();
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?#" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Playground Link</a></p>
<p>That allows <code>set()</code>, <code>delete()</code>, and <code>clear()</code> to be called but does not allow anything else:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>
foo.set(&quot;bar&quot;, &quot;yes&quot;); // allowed
foo.delete(&quot;bar&quot;);     // allowed
foo.clear();           // allowed

foo.forEach();         // not allowed
foo.entries();         // not allowed
foo.get(&quot;bar&quot;);        // not allowed
foo.has();             // not allowed

for(const entry of foo){} // not allowed
</code></pre>

Q: How do I check whether Stage Manager is enabled? A: <p>On non-sandboxed app</p>
<pre><code>let defaults = UserDefaults(suiteName: &quot;com.apple.WindowManager&quot;)!
let isStageManagerEnabled = defaults.bool(forKey: &quot;GloballyEnabled&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>For sandboxed app only a heuristic:</p>
<p>When Stage Manager is enabled there are windows with owner name &quot;WindowManager&quot;. There are some dummy 1x1 windows as well which should be filtered for more reliability.</p>
<pre><code>    @available(macOS 15.0, *)
    func isStageManagerEnabled() -&gt; Bool {
        let options: CGWindowListOption = [.optionOnScreenOnly, .excludeDesktopElements]
        guard let windowList = CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo(options, kCGNullWindowID) as? [[String: Any]] else {
            return false
        }

        let matches = windowList.filter { window in
            guard
                (window[kCGWindowOwnerName as String] as? String) == &quot;WindowManager&quot;,
                let bounds = window[kCGWindowBounds as String] as? [String: Any],
                let width = bounds[&quot;Width&quot;] as? CGFloat,
                let height = bounds[&quot;Height&quot;] as? CGFloat
            else {
                return false
            }

            return width &gt; 1 &amp;&amp; height &gt; 1
        }

        return !matches.isEmpty
    }
</code></pre>
